✅ Day 3: Users, Groups & Permissions (In-Depth)

🎯 Goals:

Understand user & group types

Learn to manage users and groups

Understand file ownership & permission relations


👤 1. Types of Users in Linux

Type	Description
Root	Superuser (Full system access)
Normal	Regular users (limited access)
System	Used for services, like mysql, nginx

💻 2. User Management Commands

Command	                         Description

adduser username	          Create user
passwd username	                Set/change user password
usermod -aG groupname username	Add user to group
deluser username	        Delete user


Example:

bash

sudo adduser ahsan

sudo passwd ahsan

👥 3. Group Management Commands

Command	                        Description

addgroup groupname	        Create a group
gpasswd -a username groupname	Add user to group
gpasswd -d username groupname	Remove user from group
delgroup groupname	        Delete group

Example:

bash

sudo addgroup devops

sudo usermod -aG devops ahsan

🗃️ 4. File Ownership with Users & Groups

Check:-

bash

ls -l file.txt

see output:-        -rw-r--r-- 1 ahsan devops 123 Apr 8  file.txt

Owner = ahsan

Group = devops

Change Owner:


sudo chown newuser:newgroup file.txt

🔐 5. /etc/passwd, /etc/group, /etc/shadow — Behind the Scenes
/etc/passwd → All user accounts

/etc/group → Group info

/etc/shadow → Encrypted passwords (root only)

View:

cat /etc/passwd | grep ahsan


🧪 Practical Tasks:--

✅ Task 1: Create a new user testuser

sudo adduser testuser

✅ Task 2: Create a group teamx and add user

sudo addgroup teamx

sudo usermod -aG teamx testuser

✅ Task 3: Create a file owned by testuser

sudo touch test.txt

sudo chown testuser:teamx test.txt

ls -l test.txt

✅ Task 4: Change permissions

sudo chmod 750 test.txt

